\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The attractor model of the number line (parameters fitted at 160~ms).} \textbf  {a}, Energy landscape over the internal evidence axis $x$: each integer contributes a memory component whose width grows with magnitude (the orange curve is the declining number-word frequency that motivates this widening), so small numbers sit in deep, isolated basins that clean up to an exact value. Figure~\ref {fig:comparison}c reports the continuous width index $n_{\mathrm  {width}}$. \textbf  {b}, Expected readout entropy $E[H]$ per presented numerosity, before (grey) and after (black) clean-up, with the attractor's expected convergence time $E[T]$ (blue, right axis); insets show example post-clean-up posteriors. Clean-up removes the residual uncertainty of the small-$n$ readout at the cost of a few settling steps; near the boundary the settling time grows and the residual entropy rises. \textbf  {c}, The selected response-time link, $\mathrm  {RT} = t_0 + \tau \,E[T]$, decomposed into basal latency and convergence time.}}{3}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:conceptual}{{1}{3}{\textbf {The attractor model of the number line (parameters fitted at 160~ms).} \textbf {a}, Energy landscape over the internal evidence axis $x$: each integer contributes a memory component whose width grows with magnitude (the orange curve is the declining number-word frequency that motivates this widening), so small numbers sit in deep, isolated basins that clean up to an exact value. Figure~\ref {fig:comparison}c reports the continuous width index $n_{\mathrm {width}}$. \textbf {b}, Expected readout entropy $E[H]$ per presented numerosity, before (grey) and after (black) clean-up, with the attractor's expected convergence time $E[T]$ (blue, right axis); insets show example post-clean-up posteriors. Clean-up removes the residual uncertainty of the small-$n$ readout at the cost of a few settling steps; near the boundary the settling time grows and the residual entropy rises. \textbf {c}, The selected response-time link, $\mathrm {RT} = t_0 + \tau \,E[T]$, decomposed into basal latency and convergence time}{figure.caption.4}{}}
