\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Model comparison and the link between landscape width and estimation acuity.} \textbf  {a}, Accuracy channel: $\Delta $AIC of each model relative to the winning power-law attractor with clean-up, over the ${\sim }53{,}000$ individual responses. Bars are coloured by model role: attractor with clean-up, matched static readout of the same wells, or external benchmark. Turning clean-up off, while retaining the same landscape and sensory parameterization but requiring no stopping-budget worsens the fit, and constant-width wells ($p=0$, $\sigma _n=\sigma _c$) worsen it further. The purely driven attractor (``Power-law wells + driven accumulator'', see Methods), whose input is the still-accumulating count rather than a completed estimate, fits far worse, because a partial count captures the wrong integer (\hyperref  [sup-sm:comparison]{Supplementary Material~2}). Weber/ANS and the bounded-optimal model of~\citet  {cheyette_unified_2020} (``Normative (C\&P)'') are shown for reference. \textbf  {b}, Response-time channel: $\Delta $AIC of the three RT links on the winning landscape, with one mapping shared across durations. Convergence time alone and the hybrid link perform almost identically, whereas entropy alone performs substantially worse. \textbf  {c}, Individual differences estimated with a bivariate hierarchical model. Non-overlapping trials informed the two constructs: $n\leq 8$ for landscape resolution and $n\geq 9$ for Weber noise. Points show empirical-Bayes participant estimates on the original scale. The inset reports the hierarchical correlation $\rho $ between the latent participant effects on $\log n_{\mathrm  {width}}$ and $\log w$, after accounting for exposure duration and finite-trial uncertainty. The model controls for exposure duration and uses sessions with both 320- and 640-ms data. }}{5}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:comparison}{{2}{5}{\textbf {Model comparison and the link between landscape width and estimation acuity.} \textbf {a}, Accuracy channel: $\Delta $AIC of each model relative to the winning power-law attractor with clean-up, over the ${\sim }53{,}000$ individual responses. Bars are coloured by model role: attractor with clean-up, matched static readout of the same wells, or external benchmark. Turning clean-up off, while retaining the same landscape and sensory parameterization but requiring no stopping-budget worsens the fit, and constant-width wells ($p=0$, $\sigma _n=\sigma _c$) worsen it further. The purely driven attractor (``Power-law wells + driven accumulator'', see Methods), whose input is the still-accumulating count rather than a completed estimate, fits far worse, because a partial count captures the wrong integer (\hyperref [sup-sm:comparison]{Supplementary Material~2}). Weber/ANS and the bounded-optimal model of~\citet {cheyette_unified_2020} (``Normative (C\&P)'') are shown for reference. \textbf {b}, Response-time channel: $\Delta $AIC of the three RT links on the winning landscape, with one mapping shared across durations. Convergence time alone and the hybrid link perform almost identically, whereas entropy alone performs substantially worse. \textbf {c}, Individual differences estimated with a bivariate hierarchical model. Non-overlapping trials informed the two constructs: $n\leq 8$ for landscape resolution and $n\geq 9$ for Weber noise. Points show empirical-Bayes participant estimates on the original scale. The inset reports the hierarchical correlation $\rho $ between the latent participant effects on $\log n_{\mathrm {width}}$ and $\log w$, after accounting for exposure duration and finite-trial uncertainty. The model controls for exposure duration and uses sessions with both 320- and 640-ms data}{figure.caption.6}{}}
